---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import architectureDiagram from "../../images/ac51f9ee-b71f-4da7-a8eb-a42f6282c64d.svg";
---

<Layout>
  <main>
    <section class="case-study">
      <div class="container case-study-layout">
        <aside class="case-study-aside">
          <p class="section-tag">
            Case Study
          </p>

          <h2>
            An AI agent that closes the daily finance reconciliation loop by verifying invoices, payment gateway settlements, and bank statements.
          </h2>

          <p>
            How I designed Finova, an autonomous finance operations agent that reconciles
            invoices, payment gateway transactions, and bank statements to surface financial
            truth across disconnected systems.
          </p>

          <div class="impact-strip">
            <div>
              <strong>60+</strong>
              <span>records reconciled per batch</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Hybrid</strong>
              <span>deterministic matching + AI reasoning</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Exceptions only</strong>
              <span>surfaced for human review</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <article class="case-study-story">
          <section>
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>Overview</h3>
            <p>
              An AI agent that automates multi-source financial reconciliation: invoices,
              payment gateway transactions, and bank statements are matched deterministically
              first, and only the transactions the rule engine cannot resolve are sent to a
              locally running Qwen 3 model (via Ollama) for analysis.
            </p>
            <p>
              Finance teams spend hours manually verifying whether payments recorded in their
              ERP have actually reached the bank. The challenge isn't generating reports - it's
              verifying financial truth across disconnected systems.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>The Problem</h3>
            <p>
              Finance teams reconcile payments across three disconnected systems: ERP invoices,
              payment gateway settlements, and bank statements. Although these records represent
              the same business transaction, they often use different identifiers, settlement
              timings, and narration formats, making manual reconciliation slow and error-prone.
            </p>

            <p>Common challenges include:</p>

            <ul>
              <li>Delayed settlements that appear days after the payment gateway reports them.</li>
              <li>Bank narrations that contain settlement IDs in inconsistent formats.</li>
              <li>Amount mismatches caused by partial or adjusted payments.</li>
              <li>Duplicate or orphan transactions requiring manual investigation.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              As transaction volume grows, finance teams spend hours matching records in
              spreadsheets instead of resolving genuine exceptions.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>The Solution</h3>
            <p>
              Finova automates financial reconciliation using a hybrid deterministic + AI
              pipeline. Instead of sending every transaction to an LLM, the system first applies
              deterministic business rules to reconcile records with high confidence. Only
              unresolved or ambiguous transactions are forwarded to a locally running Qwen 3
              model, which generates grounded explanations and helps finance teams investigate
              exceptions.
            </p>

            <p>What Finova delivers:</p>

            <ul>
              <li>Automatic reconciliation of invoices, gateway transactions, and bank statements.</li>
              <li>Explainable exception detection for delayed settlements, duplicate payments, and amount mismatches.</li>
              <li>AI-powered finance assistant that answers questions using only reconciled data.</li>
              <li>Audit-ready CSV exports for matched records and exceptions.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              This hybrid approach improves accuracy while keeping AI focused on reasoning
              rather than deterministic matching.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>Architecture Diagram</h3>

            <div class="architecture-diagram">
              <img src={architectureDiagram.src} alt="Finova system architecture: CSV uploads flow into a deterministic rule engine, splitting into auto-matched reconciled records and unresolved exceptions analyzed by a local Qwen 3 model, feeding a dashboard and CSV reports." />
            </div>

            <p>Workflow:</p>

            <ol>
              <li>Users upload invoices, gateway transactions, and bank statements as CSVs.</li>
              <li>The rule engine reconciles records using deterministic business logic.</li>
              <li>High-confidence matches are immediately added to the dashboard.</li>
              <li>Only unresolved exceptions are analyzed by the local Qwen 3 model.</li>
              <li>Finance teams receive grounded explanations, KPI dashboards, and downloadable audit reports.</li>
            </ol>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>05</span>
            <h3>AI Design Decisions</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Why a Hybrid AI Architecture?</strong> A pure LLM approach is unnecessary
              for deterministic finance operations, while a rule-only system struggles with
              ambiguous exceptions. Finova combines both to maximize accuracy and
              explainability.
            </p>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Deterministic first, AI second</dt>
                <dd>
                  Order IDs, amounts, and settlement dates are objective facts. These are
                  resolved using business rules before invoking the LLM, reducing unnecessary
                  AI usage and preventing false matches.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Grounded responses only</dt>
                <dd>
                  The AI Finance Chat never answers from general knowledge. It receives only
                  reconciled transactions and exception records as context, ensuring every
                  explanation is traceable to uploaded financial data.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Explain exceptions, not decisions</dt>
                <dd>
                  The LLM does not decide whether transactions match. It explains why a
                  transaction became an exception - for example, identifying a delayed
                  settlement or a missing bank entry - while the reconciliation outcome remains
                  deterministic and auditable.
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            <p>
              This design keeps financial reconciliation accurate, explainable,
              privacy-preserving, and reliable while using AI where it adds the most value:
              investigating exceptions rather than replacing business logic.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>06</span>
            <h3>Key Features</h3>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Batch Reconciliation</dt>
                <dd>Processes 60+ invoices, gateway settlements, and bank statements in one execution.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Cash Position Dashboard</dt>
                <dd>Displays match rate, total cash received, pending settlements, and exception count.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>AI Exception Intelligence</dt>
                <dd>
                  Explains amount mismatches, missing settlements, duplicate payments, and orphan
                  transactions with confidence scores and recommended actions.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Finance Copilot</dt>
                <dd>
                  Answers grounded questions such as "Why wasn't INV054 reconciled?" and "How much
                  cash is still pending?" using reconciled data.
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>07</span>
            <h3>Technical Architecture</h3>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Frontend</dt>
                <dd>React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Backend</dt>
                <dd>FastAPI.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Data Processing</dt>
                <dd>Pandas.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>AI Model</dt>
                <dd>Qwen 3.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>APIs</dt>
                <dd>REST.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Outputs</dt>
                <dd>Reconciled CSV & Exception CSV.</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>


<style>
.case-study {
  padding:120px 0 80px;
}

.case-study-layout {
  display:grid;
  grid-template-columns:minmax(280px, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.35fr);
  gap:72px;
  align-items:start;
}

.case-study-aside {
  position:sticky;
  top:110px;
}

.case-study-aside h2 {
  margin-bottom:22px;
}

.case-study-aside p {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  max-width:520px;
}

.impact-strip {
  display:grid;
  grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 1fr);
  border:1px solid var(--border);
  margin-top:36px;
}

.impact-strip div {
  padding:20px;
  border-right:1px solid var(--border);
}

.impact-strip div:last-child {
  border-right:0;
}

.impact-strip strong {
  display:block;
  font-size:1.5rem;
  color:var(--text-primary);
  margin-bottom:6px;
}

.impact-strip span {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  font-size:0.85rem;
}

.case-study-story {
  border-left:1px solid var(--border);
  padding-left:40px;
}

.case-study-story section {
  position:relative;
  padding-bottom:56px;
}

.case-study-story section:last-child {
  padding-bottom:0;
}

.case-study-story section > span {
  display:inline-block;
  color:var(--accent);
  font-size:0.85rem;
  font-weight:700;
  margin-bottom:12px;
}

.case-study-story h3 {
  font-size:1.45rem;
  margin-bottom:14px;
}

.case-study-story p {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  margin-bottom:16px;
}

.case-study-story ul,
.case-study-story ol {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  padding-left:18px;
  margin-bottom:16px;
}

.case-study-story li {
  margin-bottom:10px;
}

.architecture-diagram {
  border:1px solid var(--border);
  background:var(--bg-secondary);
  padding:16px;
  margin:8px 0 20px;
  display:flex;
  justify-content:center;
}

.architecture-diagram img {
  width:100%;
  max-width:420px;
  height:auto;
  border-radius:8px;
}

.case-study-story dl {
  display:grid;
  gap:18px;
  margin-top:22px;
  margin-bottom:16px;
}

.case-study-story dl div {
  border-top:1px solid var(--border);
  padding-top:16px;
}

.case-study-story dt {
  color:var(--text-primary);
  font-weight:700;
  margin-bottom:6px;
}

.case-study-story dd {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
}

@media(max-width:800px){

  .case-study {
    padding:100px 0 60px;
  }

  .case-study-layout {
    grid-template-columns:1fr;
    gap:44px;
  }

  .case-study-aside {
    position:static;
  }

  .impact-strip {
    grid-template-columns:1fr;
  }

  .impact-strip div {
    border-right:0;
    border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .impact-strip div:last-child {
    border-bottom:0;
  }

  .case-study-story {
    border-left:0;
    padding-left:0;
  }

}

@media(max-width:480px){

  .case-study {
    padding:90px 0 60px;
  }

}

@media(max-height:800px) and (min-width:801px){

  .case-study {
    padding-top:96px;
  }

  .case-study-aside {
    top:96px;
  }

}
</style>
